{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill MUST be the home of deriving an initial requirement set from existing code.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has one obligation. The `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill must be the home of deriving an initial requirement set from existing code. Under the parent `requirements.cli.skill_structure`, a topic's home is the one skill that states it in full.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_bootstrap_skill_is_home_of_deriving_an_initial_requirement_set]` covers both halves of that obligation.\n\n- **The skill states the topic.** The test reads the bootstrap skill's `SKILL.md` and calls `section` for `## Inventories`, `## Proposing requirements`, `## Tagging` and `## Report`. `section` asserts that each heading exists. Together these headings cover the workflow of deriving requirements from code.\n- **Markers are present.** `assert_home` calls `assert_phrases` with the markers from `home_markers`. For this skill they are \"every first-party runnable test or test case\", \"## Proposing requirements\" and \"Independent recount:\". These are matched against the skill's `SKILL.md` plus its `references/` files, which is how the parent defines a skill.\n- **No other skill states it.** `assert_home` loops over the other four skills in `SKILLS`. It asserts that none of them contains any of the bootstrap markers. A skill that restated this content would therefore fail the test.\n\nA skill that lacked the content, or one that duplicated it elsewhere, would fail one of these assertions. The assertions are real: `assert_text` and the `!text.contains(marker)` check both panic on failure. The markers are specific phrases, so they check substance beyond the headings.\n\nThe \"exactly one skill\" rule and the referral behaviour belong to the parent and to `skill_structure.refer`. They have their own tests and are out of scope here.\n\nI found no gap and no acknowledgments apply.",
  "references": "b02e72a8f64a2f9a1f68893267db6677c2ecf07ab8202182d024a68119169ae3",
